{"evidence":[{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Battle of Aachen was a major combat action of World War II, fought by American and German forces in and around Aachen, Germany, between 2–21 October 1944.","title":"Battle of Aachen"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The city had been incorporated into the Siegfried Line, the main defensive network on Germany's western border; the Allies had hoped to capture it quickly and advance into the industrialized Ruhr Basin.","title":"Battle of Aachen"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Although most of Aachen's civilian population was evacuated before the battle began, much of the city was destroyed and both sides suffered heavy losses.","title":"Battle of Aachen"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" It was one of the largest urban battles fought by U.S. forces in World War II, and the first city on German soil to be captured by the Allies.","title":"Battle of Aachen"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" The battle ended with a German surrender, but their tenacious defense significantly disrupted Allied plans for the advance into Germany.","title":"Battle of Aachen"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The 1st Battalion Georgia Sharpshooters were authorized by an act in April, 1862 by the Confederate Congress, and were formed in Savannah, Georgia.","title":"Georgia Sharpshooters"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The 1st Battalion Sharpshooters served at Fort McAllister defending the coast of Georgia in the Battle of Fort McAllister (1863).","title":"Georgia Sharpshooters"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" They also saw action at the Battle of Jackson, Mississippi as well as the Battle of Chickamauga, and the Chattanooga Campaign.","title":"Georgia Sharpshooters"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" As Confederate forces moved south, falling back through Atlanta.","title":"Georgia Sharpshooters"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" The Sharpshooters fought to the end of the war, their last engagement being at the Battle of Bentonville in March, 1865.","title":"Georgia Sharpshooters"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Battle of Davis's Cross Roads, also known as the Battle of Dug Gap, was fought September 10–11, 1863, in northwestern Georgia, as part of the Chickamauga Campaign of the American Civil War.","title":"Battle of Davis's Cross Roads"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It was more of a series of maneuvers and skirmishes than an actual battle and casualties were negligible.","title":"Battle of Davis's Cross Roads"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Battle of Chickamauga, fought on September 18–20, 1863 between Union and Confederate forces in the American Civil War, marked the end of a Union offensive in southeastern Tennessee and northwestern Georgia — the Chickamauga Campaign.","title":"Battle of Chickamauga"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It was the first major battle of the war fought in Georgia, the most significant Union defeat in the Western Theater, and involved the second-highest number of casualties after the Battle of Gettysburg.","title":"Battle of Chickamauga"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The following Union Army units and commanders fought in the Battle of Chickamauga of the American Civil War.","title":"Chickamauga Union order of battle"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The Confederate order of battle is listed separately.","title":"Chickamauga Union order of battle"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Order of battle compiled from the army organization during the campaign.","title":"Chickamauga Union order of battle"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The following Confederate States Army units and commanders fought in the Battle of Chickamauga of the American Civil War.","title":"Chickamauga Confederate order of battle"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The Union order of battle is listed separately.","title":"Chickamauga Confederate order of battle"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Order of battle compiled from the army organization during the campaign.","title":"Chickamauga Confederate order of battle"}],"peer_messages":["Unverified peer answer: No
